Sure! Here's a simple HTML website that displays a random joke from a list of 4 jokes when a user clicks a button:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 50px;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
        }
        #joke {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            margin-top: 20px;
            background: #fff;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 1em;
            cursor: pointer;
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>
    <button onclick="showRandomJoke()">Click for a Joke</button>
    <div id="joke"></div>

    <script>
        const jokes = [
            "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
            "What do you call a fake noodle? An impasta!",
            "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
            "What do you get when you cross a snowman and a vampire? Frostbite!"
        ];

        function showRandomJoke() {
            const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
            document.getElementById("joke").textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:
- The HTML contains a button and a `div` to display the joke.
- The JavaScript array `jokes` holds 4 jokes.
- When the button is clicked, the `showRandomJoke()` function is called.
- It selects a random joke from the array and displays it in the `div`.

You can copy and paste this code into an `.html` file and open it in a web browser to see it in action.

To change the color of the jokes to **red** using **CSS**, you can target the `#joke` element (which is the `div` that displays the joke) and set its `color` property to `red`.

Here's how you can do it:

### ✅ Updated CSS Section:

```css
#joke {
    font-size: 1.2em;
    margin-top: 20px;
    background: #fff;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    color: red; /* Add this line to change the text color to red */
}
```

### 🧠 Explanation:
- The `color: red;` property sets the text color of the joke to red.
- This applies to all jokes that are displayed in the `#joke` div.

### 📝 Result:
Now, whenever a joke is shown, it will appear in **red** color.

